    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When angiotensin I molecules arrive at the active site, they are unable to bind and be converted to angiotensin II because lisinopril is occupying the site. This is not just a matter of the angiotensin I molecules bouncing off the drug, but rather a more complex interaction.\n\nThe active site of ACE is a highly specific region, and the binding of angiotensin I is facilitated by a combination of electrostatic and hydrophobic interactions. Lisinopril, being a competitive inhibitor, has a similar shape and chemical properties to the substrate, angiotensin I. This allows it to bind to the active site with high affinity, effectively blocking the substrate from binding.\n\nThe blockade is not just a simple physical obstruction, but rather a binding event that prevents the enzyme from accessing its substrate. The binding of lisinopril to the active site is reversible, meaning that ACE can still bind to and convert angiotensin I to angiotensin II if lisinopril is removed. However, the presence of lisinopril significantly reduces the enzyme's ability to convert angiotensin I, effectively creating a \"roadblock\" that slows down the RAAS pathway.\n\nThink of",
      "source": "target",
